--TEST--
a request answers the host it named, folded the way a host mount is compared `rule:http-server/trusted-proxies-is-empty-and-empty-reads-nothing`
--CLIENT_IP--
2001:db8::1
--HEADERS--
Host: Example.Test.:8443
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The three equivalences the relevant specifications define — the case, the
// port, one trailing dot — applied once here, so a host-mounted deployment
// looks its tenant up under the same name the mount table compared.
echo Core\Request::host() ?? "none", "\n";
// The line as the peer wrote it is still readable, which is what makes the
// folded answer a second member rather than a repair of the first: a program
// that wants the port back asks for the field.
echo Core\Request::header("host") ?? "none", "\n";
// One spelling per address, whatever the peer's was: the carrier holds an
// address rather than the text of one.
echo Core\Request::clientIp() ?? "none", "\n";
// A request that named no scheme arrived over plaintext — fail-closed, since
// `https` is the claim.
echo Core\Request::scheme(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
example.test
Example.Test.:8443
2001:db8::1
http
